(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How often should a patient treated for squamous cell carcinoma be examined, and how does immunosuppression change it?","choices":["Every three months for life in all patients","Only when the patient reports a new lesion","At least annual skin and lymph-node examination, with closer intervals if high-risk or immunosuppressed","At least an annual skin examination alone, at an interval unchanged by immunosuppression"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the interval tightens rather than the examination changing. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which two clinical clues does the deck give for suspecting cutaneous T-cell lymphoma in a chronic eruption?","choices":["Annular plaques with progressive central clearing, and a positive potassium hydroxide preparation from the border","Itch out of proportion to the apparent inflammatory activity, and follicular involvement with hair loss","Itch that is entirely absent even in extensive disease, and complete sparing of the hair follicles","Sharp demarcation with thick silvery scale, and rapid complete response to a topical corticosteroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — folliculotropism discriminates from routine eczema or psoriasis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What is the prognosis of an individual actinic keratosis, and what does treatment achieve?","choices":["Individual lesions may persist, involute, recur or progress; treatment reduces lesion burden but the surrounding field remains at risk, so surveillance remains necessary","Individual lesions always progress to carcinoma if left untreated; treatment eradicates both the lesion and the surrounding field, which ends the need for any further surveillance","Individual lesions always involute spontaneously within a year; treatment is therefore cosmetic only, the field carries no residual risk, and surveillance is unnecessary","Individual lesions persist indefinitely without involuting or progressing; treatment eradicates them permanently with no recurrence and no residual field risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treating lesions does not treat the field. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does organ transplantation affect squamous cell carcinoma risk, and when do multiple tumours typically appear?","choices":["It is unchanged by transplantation, since immunosuppressive therapy does not influence keratinocyte carcinoma risk","It is common and often aggressive in transplant recipients, with multiple tumours typically emerging about 5 years after transplant","It is uncommon and usually indolent in transplant recipients, with single tumours typically emerging about 5 years after transplant","It is common and often aggressive in transplant recipients, with multiple tumours typically emerging within 6 weeks of transplant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — chronic lymphocytic leukaemia and human immunodeficiency virus also raise risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"At what Breslow thickness is sentinel lymph node biopsy offered or discussed, and what lowers that threshold?","choices":["At 4.0 mm or greater; or at 2.0 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors are present — ulceration or high mitotic rate","At any thickness once the diagnosis is confirmed, since thickness does not influence the decision to stage the nodes","At 1.0 mm or greater; or at 0.8 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors are present — ulceration, high mitotic rate, or lymphovascular invasion","At 0.8 mm or greater; or at 1.0 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors are present — ulceration, high mitotic rate, or lymphovascular invasion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — three risk factors lower the threshold. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which examination priorities does the deck list for a suspected malignant cutaneous lesion?","choices":["Good lighting and dermoscopy in every patient regardless of training; palms, soles and nails; oral mucosa in every patient; a total-body survey reserved for those who already have a skin cancer diagnosis","Wood lamp examination and potassium hydroxide preparation of any scale; palms, soles and nails; oral mucosa when tinea is possible; regional nodes in every patient regardless of risk","Dermoscopy alone, since the total-body survey, the palms, soles and nails, the oral mucosa and the regional nodes all belong to the dermatologist rather than the referring clinician","Good lighting, dermoscopy when trained, and a total-body skin survey when indicated; palms, soles and nails; oral mucosa when Kaposi sarcoma is possible; regional nodes for invasive or high-risk disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — note the oral mucosa is tied specifically to Kaposi sarcoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What warning does the deck give about oedema in Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["Marked oedema occurs only in advanced disease with many visible lesions, so its severity reliably indicates the cutaneous disease burden","Marked oedema is never a feature of Kaposi sarcoma at any stage, and its presence should prompt a search for venous thrombosis instead","Marked oedema occurs only in the classic form of the disease, and its severity can therefore be used to distinguish classic from epidemic Kaposi sarcoma","Marked oedema may occur even with few or no visible skin lesions, so oedema severity must not be used to gauge disease burden"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the skin can look almost clear while disease is extensive. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the core mechanism of actinic keratosis, and how does the deck position it relative to keratinocyte carcinoma?","choices":["Chronic ultraviolet injury producing dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is premalignant and lies on a biologic continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma, not a distinct separate entity","Chronic ultraviolet injury producing dysplastic melanocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is premalignant but a distinct entity quite separate from keratinocyte carcinoma, with no biologic continuum between them","Human papillomavirus infection of keratinocytes in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is a benign lesion lying on a continuum with verruca rather than with keratinocyte carcinoma","Chronic thermal injury producing dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of scarred skin; it is premalignant and lies on a continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma but is unrelated to ultraviolet exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a continuum, not a separate disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which basal cell carcinoma subtype is scar-like and carries the highest risk of subclinical spread?","choices":["Pigmented — a stippled lesion whose extension beyond the visible segment is clinically subtle","Morpheaform or sclerosing — a scar-like or ivory-white lesion whose extension beyond the visible pink segment is clinically subtle","Superficial — a reddish shiny scaly thin plaque whose extension beyond the visible segment is clinically subtle","Nodular — a pearly papule with central erosion whose extension beyond the visible segment is clinically subtle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the invisible extension is what makes it high-risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which indications does the deck give for Mohs surgery in basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["Eyelids, nasolabial folds, canthi, external ear and temple; recurrent tumours; a tissue-sparing or cosmetic need; and low-risk histology — superficial or nodular disease","The trunk, back, chest and shoulders; first presentations only; a purely cosmetic need; and any pigmented lesion whatever its histology","Any lesion in a patient over 65; any lesion larger than 6 mm; any lesion present more than a year; and any lesion the patient finds unsightly","Eyelids, nasolabial folds, canthi, external ear and temple; recurrent tumours; a tissue-sparing or cosmetic need; and aggressive histology — morpheaform, micronodular or infiltrative"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — four groupings, with the histologic subtypes named. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which history elements does the deck ask you to elicit?","choices":["Onset and change; sunburns, occupational exposure and tanning-bed use; chronic scars and radiation sites; family history — but not immunosuppression or transplant status, which do not alter cutaneous malignancy risk","Onset and change, pain or pruritus; occupational and recreational ultraviolet exposure and tanning-bed use; immunosuppression and family history — but not prior skin cancer, which does not predict a second primary","Onset and change, bleeding or non-healing, pain or pruritus; sunburns, occupational or recreational ultraviolet exposure and tanning-bed use; prior skin cancer, immunosuppression or transplant, human immunodeficiency virus risk; chronic scars, wounds or radiation sites; family history","Onset and change, bleeding or non-healing, pain or pruritus; sunburns, occupational exposure and tanning-bed use; chronic scars, wounds and radiation sites; family history — but not prior skin cancer, immunosuppression, transplant or human immunodeficiency virus status, which the dermatologist obtains at the time of biopsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — note chronic scars and radiation sites among them. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Describe NODULAR basal cell carcinoma, including the manoeuvre that accentuates its vessels.","choices":["A papule or nodule with central erosion, growing rapidly over weeks to 1 to 2 cm or larger, with a pearly quality and telangiectasias accentuated by pressing the lesion firmly","A reddish shiny scaly thin plaque on the back or chest, growing slowly, with a thready pearly border accentuated by stretching the skin","A scar-like ivory-white plaque with subtle extension beyond the visible segment, accentuated by stretching the skin","A papule or nodule with central erosion, growing slowly over years to 1 to 2 cm or larger, with a pearly or translucent quality and visible telangiectasias accentuated by STRETCHING the skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — stretching brings the telangiectasias out. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does Kaposi sarcoma look like, and which examination does the deck call essential when it is suspected?","choices":["Red or purple macules, plaques or nodules on skin or mucous membranes; ORAL examination is essential, since hard palate lesions are common and may be the presenting site","Red or purple macules, plaques or nodules on skin only; NAIL examination is essential, since subungual lesions are common and may be the presenting site","Pearly translucent papules with telangiectasias; ocular examination is essential, since eyelid lesions are the usual presenting site","Erythematous scaly patches and plaques on the trunk; scalp examination is essential, since follicular involvement is the presenting sign"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the mouth may be where it declares itself. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which clinical forms of Kaposi sarcoma does the deck describe?","choices":["Patch, plaque, tumour and erythrodermic disease, each occurring in a different population and each representing a successive stage of the same underlying process","Classic in older men with a chronic course; endemic, often aggressive in young Black men in equatorial Africa; iatrogenic with immunosuppressive therapy; and epidemic in acquired immunodeficiency","Superficial, nodular, pigmented and morpheaform disease, each occurring in a different population and each with a different histologic behaviour and course","Superficial spreading, lentigo maligna, nodular and acral lentiginous disease, each occurring in a different population and each with a different growth pattern and course"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — four forms, each with a different population and course. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which findings does the deck list as urgent referral triggers in the nail unit?","choices":["Only a subungual mass confirmed on imaging, since clinical features such as pigment, widening, dystrophy or bleeding cannot themselves indicate urgency","New or changing single-digit longitudinal melanonychia, proximal widening, Hutchinson sign, or nail dystrophy with pigment","Longitudinal pigmented bands present in several nails since childhood, unchanged in width, colour, thickness and spacing over many years, with no nail dystrophy","Any nail change at all in a patient over 65, and any nail that has been abnormal for more than a month, whatever the appearance or the number of digits involved"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the list also includes an unexplained subungual mass, persistent ulceration or bleeding, and a chronic wart or infection that fails appropriate therapy. That failed-wart history is the one most easily dismissed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What follow-up schedule does the deck specify after squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["No scheduled follow-up once the lesion has been completely excised with clear histologic margins","At least annual skin AND lymph-node examination, at closer intervals for high-risk or immunosuppressed patients","At least annual skin examination alone, with lymph nodes assessed only once metastasis is suspected clinically","Examination every five years, at closer intervals only for patients who have had more than ten tumours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the node examination is part of the routine, not an extra. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the most important distinction to make when assessing an actinic keratosis, and what raises that concern?","choices":["Solar lentigo — raised when a lesion is uniformly pigmented, flat, non-indurated, painless and stable over many years","Early squamous cell carcinoma or carcinoma in situ — raised when a lesion is thick, indurated, ulcerated, enlarging, painful, bleeding, persistent or recurrent","Seborrheic keratosis — raised when a lesion is thick, waxy, stuck-on, sharply demarcated, enlarging, persistent or recurrent","Superficial basal cell carcinoma — raised when a lesion is pearly with rolled borders, visible telangiectasias, ulceration, bleeding or slow enlargement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that list is the biopsy trigger. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the Hutchinson sign in the nail unit, and what should it prompt?","choices":["Periungual pigment extending onto the proximal nail fold — highly concerning for nail unit melanoma, prompting urgent expert evaluation regardless of other features","Pigment confined strictly beneath the nail plate without any periungual extension — reassuring, and allowing routine follow-up whatever the other features","Vesicles on the tip or side of the nose — concerning for ocular involvement in herpes zoster, prompting same-day ophthalmology review","A small red-blue subungual focus with paroxysmal pain and cold sensitivity — concerning for a glomus tumour, prompting imaging and specialist review"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it outranks the rest of the picture. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What is the classic triad of a glomus tumour?","choices":["A longitudinal pigmented band with proximal widening, blurred borders and nail splitting","A rapidly growing friable bleeding papule with an epithelial collarette","A small red-blue subungual focus with severe paroxysmal pain, exquisite point tenderness, and cold sensitivity","A verrucous periungual plaque with subungual hyperkeratosis, onycholysis and painless bleeding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the nail may look nearly normal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What is the overall prognosis of basal cell carcinoma, and where does its morbidity come from?","choices":["Usually slow-growing and highly curable when treated early; morbidity comes from local destruction, recurrence, delayed diagnosis and anatomically complex sites, with metastatic disease rare but serious","Usually rapidly growing and rarely curable even when treated early; morbidity comes chiefly from early distant metastasis to lung and liver, with local destruction uncommon","Usually slow-growing and highly curable when treated early; morbidity comes chiefly from nodal metastasis, which occurs in 3 to 7% of cases, rather than from local destruction","Usually slow-growing but uniformly fatal without systemic therapy; morbidity comes from delayed hedgehog inhibitor treatment rather than from local destruction or recurrence"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the danger is local destruction rather than spread. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which red flags does the deck list for squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Rapid growth, pain, bleeding, ulceration, induration, fixation, or palpable regional nodes","Slow growth, painlessness, a stable size, soft consistency, free mobility, and no palpable nodes","Pruritus, fine scaling, symmetry of the lesion, sharp demarcation, and a waxy stuck-on surface","Sandpaper texture, tenderness on palpation, flesh-coloured hue, and a size under 6 mm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — seven features. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which chemoprevention does the deck give for squamous cell carcinoma, at what dose, and with what effect?","choices":["Nicotinamide 500 mg orally twice daily, reducing new squamous cell carcinoma development by approximately 30% in high-risk patients","Nicotinamide 500 mg orally twice daily, reducing new squamous cell carcinoma development by approximately 20% in high-risk patients","Oral acitretin 25 mg daily, reducing new squamous cell carcinoma development by approximately 30% in high-risk patients","Topical fluorouracil twice daily, reducing new squamous cell carcinoma development by approximately 50% in high-risk patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the corresponding figure for basal cell carcinoma is about 20%. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the early morphology of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, and what is it commonly mistaken for?","choices":["Localised or generalised erythematous patches or scaly plaques, usually on the trunk and frequently larger than 5 cm; it may resemble psoriasis, eczema or tinea","Localised pearly papules with telangiectasias, usually on the face and frequently smaller than 5 mm; it may resemble sebaceous hyperplasia","Red or purple macules and nodules, usually on the lower limbs and oral mucosa; it may resemble bacillary angiomatosis","Rough sandpaper papules on sun-exposed skin, usually under 6 mm; it may resemble seborrheic keratosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — which is why diagnosis is often delayed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What does the deck say about amelanotic nail melanoma?","choices":["It occurs only in patients who already have multiple pigmented bands across several nails, so a single unexplained lesion can be observed","It is a benign variant of longitudinal melanonychia requiring no biopsy, provided the nail plate itself remains intact and unsplit","It may appear red, pink, eroded or mass-like without a dark band, and the absence of pigment does NOT exclude melanoma","It always shows at least a faint pigmented band somewhere in the nail, so the complete absence of pigment reliably excludes melanoma from the differential"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a pigment-free nail lesion can still be melanoma, so consider biopsy for any unexplained, progressive single-nail lesion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What is the initial diagnostic test for a lesion suspicious for melanoma?","choices":["Dermoscopy alone, with biopsy reserved for lesions that change","Serum lactate dehydrogenase and whole-body imaging","Biopsy or excision to confirm the diagnosis","Sentinel lymph node biopsy before any skin sampling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — tissue is required. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What does the deck say you should do before assigning a diagnosis to a skin lesion?","choices":["Photograph the lesion and defer all description — type, colour, texture, border, size, distribution and evolution — until the dermatology referral has been accepted","Biopsy every pigmented lesion before any description of type, colour, texture, border or evolution, since morphology is unreliable in premalignant disease","Characterise it systematically — primary lesion type, colour, surface texture, border definition, size, distribution, palpability, ulceration, bleeding, induration and temporal evolution","Assign the most likely diagnosis first, then look for the features that confirm it — colour, texture, border, size and evolution — and discard those that do not fit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — describe before naming. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which systemic agents are first-line in Kaposi sarcoma, and what does the deck say about combining them with antiretroviral therapy?","choices":["Liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel; antiretroviral therapy alone is more effective than the combination in advanced disease","Vismodegib and sonidegib; antiretroviral therapy plus a hedgehog inhibitor is more effective than antiretroviral therapy alone","Romidepsin and vorinostat; antiretroviral therapy plus a histone deacetylase inhibitor is more effective than antiretroviral therapy alone","Liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel; antiretroviral therapy plus chemotherapy is more effective than antiretroviral therapy alone in advanced disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — combination beats antiretroviral therapy alone once disease is advanced. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What does the deck say about what sentinel lymph node biopsy achieves?","choices":["It replaces re-excision of the primary lesion when nodes are negative","It is a STAGING procedure, and may not itself improve overall survival","It is a THERAPEUTIC procedure that reliably improves overall survival","It is a screening procedure used before any biopsy of the primary lesion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — an important expectation to set with a patient. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What self-examination does the deck ask melanoma patients to perform, and which sites must be included?","choices":["Annual self-examination using the recognition mnemonic alone, limited to the sun-exposed skin of the face, arms and upper chest","Monthly self-examination of the trunk and limbs only, since acral, nail and scalp melanoma are too rare to warrant routine checking","No self-examination at all, since surveillance of the skin, scalp, nails and acral sites is entirely the specialist team's responsibility","Monthly self-examination using the recognition mnemonic and ugly-duckling principles, including the scalp, back, palms, soles and nails"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the acral and scalp sites are easy to miss. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which factors does the deck list as amplifying actinic keratosis risk?","choices":["Young age, intermittent ultraviolet exposure with indoor occupation, no prior actinic keratosis or keratinocyte carcinoma, female sex with full scalp hair, and a fully competent immune system","Obesity, diabetes with vascular disease, incontinence and immobility in a hospitalised patient, and recent antibiotic exposure","Atopic dermatitis, asthma and allergic rhinitis in a young adult, with a family history of atopy and frequent emollient use","Advanced age, cumulative ultraviolet exposure with outdoor work or recreation, prior actinic keratosis or keratinocyte carcinoma, male sex with bald scalp exposure, and immunosuppression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — five amplifiers. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"}]};
